'''Annie Pepple House'''

It had been founded by the legendary King who ruled Bonny from 1792 to 1830 and who established the pre-eminence of the city-state in the Eastern Delta. Named '''Hanno''' by European traders and corrupted by Bonny people to '''Anna or Annie''' (hence Opubo’s houses became known as '''Annie Pepple'''). Opubo was the third son of '''Perekule''' (also called Pelekule), the '''first King Pepple of Bonny'''. Opubo succeeded his deceased brother Fubara ('''2nd King Pepple''') to become the '''3rd King Pepple''' in 1792. The first King Pepple was the Wealthiest and most powerful man in Bonny and had set up his 3 sons with their own Canoe-houses as befitting Kings.:

1st son '''Ibulu (Best) Pepple''' Canoe-house, Passed on succession to the thrown. 
2nd son '''Fubara (Manilla ) Pepple''' Canoe-house, succeeded Perekule as King of Bonny.  
3rd son '''Opubo (Annie) Pepple''' Canoe-house, succeeded Fubara as '''King of Bonny.''' 

Jack Jaja Annie Pepple became the head of the Royal house of Opubo (Annie) Pepple.
After the bonny civil war in Bonny, Jaja immerged as the supreme leader and paramount chief of all the chiefs that left Bonny with him to find a new place to settle, this settlement was then named Opubo in memory of the Great Bonny ruler of modern times and founder of the Annie Pepple houses. The name of the settlement Opubo was later to be corrupted by Europeans to Opobo which became popular and is still used today. 
Jaja was crowned King Jaja (1st) of Opobo, thus creating the '''Jaja''' Dynasty of Opobo. 

King Jaja (1st) too had 3 sons:
1st son '''Saturday Jaja''', Passed on succession to the thrown. 
2nd son '''Sunday Jaja''', succeeded King Jaja (1st) as King Jaja (2nd) of Opobo.  
3rd son '''Arthur MacPepple-Jaja''', succeeded King Jaja (2nd) as King Jaja (3rd) of Opobo.  
''' Douglas, MacPepple-Jaja''', succeeded King Jaja (3rd) as King Jaja (4th) of Opobo.  
'''Dandyson, Douglas, Jaja''' succeeded King Jaja (4rd) as King Jaja (5th) of Opobo. 
His Royal Majesty King Jaja (5th) is the reigning Monarch of the Opobo (Oil Rivers) Kingdom today.

Information supplied from extracts from book '''King Jaja of the Niger Delta: His life and times 1821-1891 S.J.S. Cookey'''
